finding: Blocking — index.ts:483–486 retains acknowledged row states nobody uses. Record ticks 0 through N before any ACK, so every delta names base 0. Then deliver separate ordered ACKs 0 through N−1 without recording. With only frame N pending, _bases still has N entries (0 through N−1), although only 0 and N−1 are needed. A draining seat can reaccumulate O(backlog) row states even as pending shrinks; the room cap limits the initial backlog but does not make the claimed lifetime true. The silent-seat test never reaches this path. Blocking — replication-retention.node.ts:35–64 asserts a delta of the process-wide JSC heap, not memory owned by the declared subject. Full GC does not remove unrelated reachable objects or pin runtime warmup/cache growth and backing-store accounting. The verdict therefore depends on undeclared process/runtime state, contrary to the Code test rule. Keep the byte result as a measurement or check ownership directly; the test also does not check return to baseline after disposal.
